---
import BlogLayout from "../../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import { blogPosts } from "../../../lib/blogPosts";

const post = blogPosts.find((p) => p.slug === "power-of-attorney-guide")!;
---

<BlogLayout
  locale="en"
  path="/en/blog/power-of-attorney-guide"
  altPath="/blog/power-of-attorney-guide"
  title={post.titleEn}
  description={post.excerptEn}
  publishDate={post.publishDate}
>
  <p>
    A power of attorney (PoA) is a formal document authorizing another person (the agent) to act on your behalf in
    specific matters — administrative, financial, or legal. In Saudi Arabia, a PoA is notarized through the Ministry
    of Justice's official platforms, and it can also be requested through a licensed lawyer who drafts its wording
    to fit the intended purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>Individual power of attorney</h2>
  <p>
    This is the most common form: one individual authorizes another to act on a specific personal matter, such as
    selling a property, collecting dues, or following up on a government procedure. Its wording is usually simpler,
    with directly and clearly stated authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Corporate power of attorney</h2>
  <p>
    When the principal is a company rather than an individual, a PoA differs in two ways. First, the authority of
    the person signing on the company's behalf must be established (typically whoever holds signing authority per
    the commercial registration or articles of incorporation). Second, the granted authority is often broader or
    tied specifically to company business (signing contracts, opening bank accounts, representing the company before
    government entities) — which calls for more detailed wording to avoid any ambiguity in the scope of authority
    granted.
  </p>

  <h2>General points worth noting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Define the granted authority clearly — a broad, general PoA carries more risk than a purpose-specific one.</li>
    <li>Check the expiry date if one exists, and whether it can be revoked if you need to later.</li>
    <li>Keep an official, notarized copy, not an unnotarized scan, especially for financial PoAs.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This article is general information only. To have a PoA drafted precisely for your situation — individual or
    corporate — you can submit a direct notarization request under the "Notarization Services" category on the
    platform, and a licensed lawyer will prepare it.
  </p>
</BlogLayout>
